{"n":46,"paragraphs":["Swamiji explained that in India both Muslims and Hindus regarded the fox as very unclean. Likewise, if a dog touched any food, that food had to be thrown away; nobody could eat it.","In a certain Muslim household, a poor man had made himself a really fine meal. Before he could eat, a fox slipped in, grabbed a bite from the table and ran off. Now the whole feast was impure and he could not touch it. Upset, he went to the mullah, the local priest, and poured out his trouble. He had been so looking forward to that feast, and now this fox had ruined everything. What could be done?","The mullah thought for a moment and came up with exactly one answer. Get a dog, he said, and let it eat a little from the same plate. Dogs and foxes are forever fighting, so whatever the fox left behind and whatever the dog left behind would both end up in the man's stomach, and the two impurities would cancel each other out. The food would be pure again.","Swamiji said we are in a very similar situation. We are caught in one illusion, that we are imperfect. So we take up a second idea, that we must practise to become perfect. The second chases out the first, just as you use one thorn to pull out another thorn stuck in your skin, and then throw both away."],"learn":"Sometimes a helpful tool, a rule or a habit is only meant to remove a problem, not to become a new burden. Use the second thorn to get the first one out, and do not forget to drop both once the job is done.","source":"Vol. 3, pp. 16–17","images":["data:image/jpeg;base64,"]}